Take the timebase from CNTFRQ_EL0 instead of the Raspberry Pi's 1 MHz.

pe_identify_machine.c picks the clock rates by SoC name, and this board
answers to "bcm2837-io" because that is the machine configuration the kernel
is built with. The branch is the Raspberry Pi 3's:

    } else if (!strcmp(gPESoCDeviceType, "bcm2837-io")) {
            mclk = 1200000000;
            hclk = mclk / 4;
            pclk = hclk / 2;
            tclk = 1000000;
    } else {
            use_dt = 1;
    }

Matching a name means use_dt stays clear, so the device tree's
timebase-frequency is never read: there is no way to correct this from the
ADT, only from here.

The Exynos 7570's architected counter does not run at 1 MHz. It runs at
about 26 MHz, so the kernel believes twenty-six seconds have passed for
every real one. Nothing announces itself; everything that measures a
duration is simply wrong by that factor, and the damage shows up as
unrelated-looking faults:

    process mediaserverd[...] denied attempt to escape CPU monitor
    send_resource_violation(CPU wakes, ...): error 0x10000004

Both are wall clock windows. The CPU monitor asks how much processor time a
process used inside a window, and the window closes before the process has
had the share it is allowed, so a program that did nothing wrong is killed
for it. The wake rate monitor counts wakes per unit time against a clock
running twenty-six times fast. Above them, every timeout in userland is
short by the same factor, which is a poor foundation for bringing up a
system whose startup is a sequence of handshakes.

CNTFRQ_EL0 is the architected answer to this question and the bootloader has
already programmed it before the shim runs. This reads it, and takes it only
when it is a believable counter rate -- at least 1 MHz, at most 1 GHz.
Outside that the Raspberry Pi value stays, so a board whose firmware leaves
the register at zero behaves exactly as it does today.

It was already known. The prose of iokit-secure-root-no-platform-kext.patch
records it as a separate problem, noted there because it explained why a
thirty second wait looked instant. This is that separate problem.
diff -ruN a/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c	2026-10-24 11:54:22.014469342 +0300
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c	2026-11-07 19:47:19.088806656 +0300
@@ -101,6 +101,40 @@
 		hclk = mclk / 4;
 		pclk = hclk / 2;
 		tclk = 1000000;
+#if defined(__arm64__)
+		/*
+		 * The 1 MHz above is the Raspberry Pi 3's. This board only
+		 * borrows the BCM2837 machine configuration; its architected
+		 * counter runs at its own rate, and because the SoC name
+		 * matches here the device tree's timebase-frequency is never
+		 * consulted -- use_dt stays clear.
+		 *
+		 * Getting it wrong is not a small error. Every duration the
+		 * kernel computes is scaled by the ratio, so on a counter that
+		 * actually ticks around 26 MHz a deadline arrives twenty-six
+		 * times too early: the CPU monitor closes its wall clock
+		 * window before a process has had its share and reports a
+		 * violation that did not happen, the wake rate monitor does
+		 * the same, and every timeout in userland above them is short
+		 * by the same factor.
+		 *
+		 * CNTFRQ_EL0 is the architected answer and the bootloader has
+		 * already programmed it. It is taken only when it is a
+		 * believable counter rate; anything else leaves the Raspberry
+		 * Pi value in place, so a board that does not set the register
+		 * is no worse off than before.
+		 */
+		{
+			uint64_t cntfrq = 0;
+
+			__asm__ volatile ("mrs %0, CNTFRQ_EL0" : "=r" (cntfrq));
+			if (cntfrq >= 1000000ULL && cntfrq <= 1000000000ULL) {
+				tclk = (uint32_t)cntfrq;
+			}
+			kprintf("pe_identify_machine: CNTFRQ_EL0 %llu Hz, "
+			    "timebase %u Hz\n", cntfrq, tclk);
+		}
+#endif /* __arm64__ */
 	} else {
 		use_dt = 1;
 	}
